"The postoperative prognosis is guided by several documented factors. Favorable findings include negative surgical margins, absence of seminal vesicle invasion, absence of lymphovascular invasion, and no lymph node metastasis. The pathological stage was pT2c, and the surgical grade was ISUP grade group 3. Countervailing factors include the preoperative PSA of 19. The biopsy findings showed a Gleason 3+4 pattern, which differs from the prostatectomy specimen's Gleason 4+3. Nodal status was documented as no lymph node metastasis.\n\nThe uncertainty in the prognosis is widened by the absence of previous notes, which prevents assessment of postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory. Information regarding postoperative PSA levels and its trend would significantly revise this prognosis. The current heuristic sensitivity range for recurrence is between 47 and 78 months. The fixed estimated horizon is 62.79 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 47.09–78.49 months; its coverage has not been validated."
